Queues Theory
==============

Core Idea:
- First-In, First-Out (FIFO) data structure
- Enqueue adds to the back, Dequeue removes from the front
- Elements are processed in the order they arrive
- Think of it as a line at a store -- first person in line gets served first


Types of Queues:
-----------------

Simple Queue (FIFO):
  - Standard queue -- enqueue at back, dequeue from front
  - Implementation: collections.deque in Python (O(1) both ends)

Double-Ended Queue (Deque):
  - Can add/remove from BOTH front and back
  - Used for: sliding window maximum, palindrome checking
  - Implementation: collections.deque in Python

Priority Queue / Heap:
  - Elements dequeued by priority, not insertion order
  - Used for: Dijkstra's, k-th largest, merge k sorted lists
  - Implementation: heapq module in Python (min-heap by default)
  - See the heap problems in this folder for implementation details

Circular Queue:
  - Front and back wrap around using modulo arithmetic
  - Used for: fixed-size buffers, round robin scheduling


When to Use Queues:
--------------------
- BFS (Breadth-First Search) -- exploring nodes level by level in graphs/trees
- Level-order tree traversal -- visiting nodes level by level
- Task scheduling -- processing jobs in the order they arrive (print queue, CPU scheduling)
- Message/event handling -- processing events or messages in arrival order
- Rate limiting / throttling -- managing requests in order
- Buffering -- streaming data, IO buffers, keyboard input buffers
- Order processing systems -- tickets, customer service lines, food orders
- Producer-consumer problems -- one side adds data, the other side processes it in order
- Cache eviction (FIFO-based) -- removing the oldest cached item first


When NOT to Use Queues:
------------------------
- Need LIFO order -- use a stack (undo operations, backtracking)
- Need random access by index -- use an array
- Need priority-based processing -- use a priority queue / heap
- Need fast lookups by key -- use a hash map
- Need to reverse elements -- a stack is more natural
- Need to sort or search frequently -- arrays, trees, or heaps work better
- Order of processing doesn't matter -- simpler structures like sets or lists may suffice
- Need to remove from the middle frequently -- linked list or other structure is better


Simple Test:
------------
Ask yourself:
  "Do I need to process elements in the exact order they arrived?"

  YES -> Queue is likely the right choice
  NO  -> Think stack (LIFO), heap (priority), or hashmap (lookup)


Queue vs Stack vs Heap:
------------------------
Need                                       |  Use
------------------------------------------|--------------------
Process in arrival order (FIFO)            |  Queue
Process most recent first (LIFO)           |  Stack
Process by priority (highest/lowest)       |  Heap / Priority Queue
Process from both ends                     |  Deque
BFS traversal                              |  Queue
DFS traversal                              |  Stack
K-th largest/smallest                      |  Heap
Sliding window min/max                     |  Monotonic Deque
